# M3-the-held-file-borrows-the-computers-sentence
#
# TWO VARIANTS WERE RUN, because the first fired a different assertion than the one this
# mutation is aimed at.
#
# VARIANT A — the whole copy replaced by `.unreachable`'s:
#   RED: [failed] A recording held on this phone is never blamed on the computer:
#        the held-file copy does not say where the file is
#   (fires the SUBTITLE assertion, not the population one.)
#
# VARIANT B — the copy still says where the file is, and ALSO blames the computer. This is the
# realistic version: a well-meaning edit that adds the old sentence back "for context".
#   RED: [failed] A recording held on this phone is never blamed on the computer:
#        a file on this iPhone was reported with the computer's line: 'Can't reach your computer'
#   The assertion is over the POPULATION — every line of `.unreachable` and `.unavailable` — so
#   it cannot be satisfied by remembering one string, and the same check asserts
#   `Set(everyLine).count == everyLine.count` so two outcomes cannot collapse into one sentence.
#
--- a/Sources/FoundryXKit/AudioPlaybackPresentation.swift
+++ b/Sources/FoundryXKit/AudioPlaybackPresentation.swift
@@ -124,8 +124,8 @@
         case .heldButUnreadable:
-            return Copy(title: "Couldn't play this recording",
-                        subtitle: "Its file is on this iPhone, so your computer isn't needed.")
+            return Copy(title: "Couldn't play this recording",
+                        subtitle: "Its file is on this iPhone. Can't reach your computer.")
         }
